    id: "function-structure",
    subject: "cpp",
    title: "Function Structure",
    content: "Anatomy of a well-structured function:\n\n// Function signature (return type + name + parameters)\ndouble calculateAverage(const vector<int>& numbers) {\n    // Guard clause (handle edge cases)\n    if (numbers.empty()) {\n        return 0.0;\n    }\n\n    // Main logic\n    double sum = 0;\n    for (int num : numbers) {\n        sum += num;\n    }\n\n    // Return result\n    return sum / numbers.size();\n}\n\nFunction Organization:\n1. Guard clause: handle edge cases first\n2. Main logic: the core algorithm\n3. Return: the result\n\nSingle Responsibility Principle:\nEach function should do ONE thing well.\n\n// Bad: does too many things\nvoid processStudent(int id, string name, double gpa) {\n    // validates, calculates, prints, saves to file...\n}\n\n// Good: each function does one thing\nbool validateStudent(int id, string name);\ndouble calculateHonors(double gpa);\nvoid displayStudent(int id, string name, double gpa);\nvoid saveStudent(int id, string name, double gpa);\n\nFunction Length Guidelines:\n- 1-20 lines: ideal\n- 20-50 lines: acceptable\n- 50+ lines: consider splitting\n\nNaming Functions:\n- Use verbs: calculateArea, printReport, getMax\n- Be descriptive: calculateStudentAverage (not calc)\n- Follow conventions: camelCase or snake_case (be consistent)",
