    id: "arrays",
    subject: "cpp",
    title: "Arrays",
    content: "An array is a collection of elements of the same type stored in contiguous memory.\n\nDeclaring Arrays:\nint scores[5];           // 5 integers (uninitialized)\nint nums[5] = {1,2,3,4,5};  // Initialized\nint zeros[5] = {};       // All zeros\nint partial[5] = {1,2};  // {1,2,0,0,0}\n\nAccessing Elements:\nint arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\narr[0];   // 10 (first element)\narr[4];   // 50 (last element)\narr[5];   // OUT OF BOUNDS! Undefined behavior\n\nArray Size:\nint arr[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\nsizeof(arr);       // 20 bytes (5 * 4 bytes per int)\nsizeof(arr[0]);    // 4 bytes (size of one int)\n\n// Modern C++ (preferred)\nint size = std::size(arr);  // 5\n\n// Or compute\nint size = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);  // 5\n\nModifying Elements:\nint arr[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\narr[0] = 100;   // {100, 2, 3, 4, 5}\narr[2] = 99;    // {100, 2, 99, 4, 5}\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Array index out of bounds (no runtime check!)\n- Using variable index in C-style array: arr[i] is OK, but arr[n] where n is not constant is fine in C++\n- Forgetting arrays are 0-indexed\n- Comparing arrays: arr1 == arr2 doesn't work (compares addresses)",
