    id: "references",
    subject: "cpp",
    title: "References",
    content: "A reference is an alias for an existing variable.\n\nDeclaring References:\nint x = 10;\nint& ref = x;  // ref is another name for x\nref = 20;      // x is now 20\n\nReference Rules:\n- Must be initialized when declared\n- Cannot be null\n- Cannot be reassigned to refer to another variable\n\nReference vs Pointer:\nint x = 10, y = 20;\n\n// Pointer\nint* ptr = &x;  // OK: pointer to x\nptr = &y;       // OK: reassign to point to y\n*ptr = 30;      // y is now 30\n\n// Reference\nint& ref = x;   // OK: ref aliases x\nref = 30;       // x is now 30 (not reassignment!)\n// int& ref2 = y;  // Error: cannot reassign reference\n\nPass by Reference (Function Parameters):\nvoid increment(int& x) {\n    x++;  // Modifies original variable\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 5;\n    increment(a);  // a is now 6\n}\n\nUse Cases:\n1. Function parameters (modify original, avoid copying)\n2. Range-based for loops (avoid copying)\n3. Operator overloading\n\nWhen to Use References vs Pointers:\nReference: when null isn't meaningful, when you need an alias\nPointer: when null is meaningful, when you need to reassign",
